      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}z.$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\text{d}z & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{4}{{z}^{2}}\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{4}{z})]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{4}{a}+\\frac{4}{2}) \\\\  & =0+2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
