    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-350333",
      "knowledge_point_id": "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}\\frac{12}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{7}}}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{s}^{-7/3}\\,\\text{d}s \\\\  & =12{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{4}{s}^{-4/3}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{s}^{4}}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{a}^{4}}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{4}}}) \\\\  & =-9(0-1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
