    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-350324",
      "knowledge_point_id": "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{16}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =16{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =16{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[(-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-2})]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-8\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-8(0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
