      "math_academy_id": "q-20881",
      "knowledge_point_id": "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-3}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}]}_{a}^{-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{(-3)^{3}}+\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{27}+\\frac{1}{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{27}+0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
