    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-350547",
      "knowledge_point_id": "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{({x}^{7}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $u={x}^{7}-3.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7{x}^{6}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=7{x}^{6}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{7}-3$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-\\infty $ | $-1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-\\infty $ | $-4$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{7{x}^{6}}{({x}^{7}-3)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
