    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-152079",
      "knowledge_point_id": "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to -\\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $-1$ | $-\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{\\infty }^{2}\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{x}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{u}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-2}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(0-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
