    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-350537",
      "knowledge_point_id": "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-1}\\frac{10}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x & =10{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-1}{x}^{-6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{-5}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}]}_{a}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-1)^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{5}}] \\\\  & =-2\\cdot (-1-0) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
