  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-511",
      "knowledge_point_id": "6ef2ab87-f78b-5cbd-9be4-0c1ac3448d89",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_2^\\infty \\dfrac { 8}{t^5}  \\,\\textrm{d}t.$",
      "worked_solution": "We proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}\\frac{8}{{t}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{2}^{a}{t}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{-4}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{{t}^{4}}]}_{2}^{a} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{2}^{4}}) \\\\  & =-2(0-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10717",
      "knowledge_point_id": "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we substitute $u=x+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x} = 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u \\to \\infty$ when $x \\to \\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\int_0^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\n\\int_1^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{u^2} \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{a}-\\frac{1}{1}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (0-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-512",
      "knowledge_point_id": "39a476b5-eacc-59c1-9d16-fc0675e3bc12",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Lower Limit",
      "problem": "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that, this time, it's the *lower* limit of integration that's unbounded.  \n\nFirst, we substitute $u=x-4.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}= 1\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\textrm{d}u =\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to -\\infty$ when $x\\to -\\infty,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x-4$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-\\infty$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $-4$ | $-\\infty$ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{0}    \\dfrac{1}{(x-4)^2} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n=\\int_{-\\infty}^{-4} \\dfrac{1}{u^2}  \\,\\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to-\\infty.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{1}{(x-4)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{1}{a}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-513",
      "knowledge_point_id": "dc6d9b69-0459-515a-ba7b-c62cab96ca00",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Divergent Improper Integrals",
      "problem": "Which of the following improper integrals are divergent?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_1^\\infty \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x} }\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{-\\infty}^{-2} \\frac{1}{(1+x)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}x$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^\\infty \\dfrac{1}{x^3} \\,\\textrm{d}x$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's work out each integral.\n\n- For the first integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[2{x}^{1/2}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[\\sqrt{x}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\sqrt{b}-1] \\\\  & =\\infty ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral diverges.\n\n- For the second integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-2}\\frac{1}{(1+x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{1+x}]}_{a}^{-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[-\\frac{1}{1-2}+\\frac{1}{1+a}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{1+a}+1] \\\\  & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\n- For the third integral, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2({1}^{2})}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the integral is convergent (not divergent).\n\nIn conclusion, only integral I is divergent.",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
